<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/BBA26-FigureSPM.1> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Overview of the business and biodiversity assessment. Businesses both depend on and impact biodiversity creating risks and opportunities. Methods exist to measure business impacts and dependencies. These can be used to inform actions at all decision-making levels (i.e. portfolio corporate value chain and operations). Businesses including financial institutions working with Governments and financial and other actors including civil society and Indigenous Peoples and local communities can create an enabling environment. This can be achieved through changes in policy legal and regulatory frameworks (“policy and legal”) economic and financial systems (“economics and finance”) social values norms and culture (“values and norms”) technology and data and capacity and knowledge {KM10}." ;
    dcterms:identifier "FigureSPM.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/BBA26-FigureSPM.2> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Creating an enabling environment would align what is beneficial for businesses with what is beneficial for biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people. Business activities impacting biodiversity outcomes are influenced by policy legal and regulatory frameworks; economic and financial systems; social values norms and culture; technology and data; and capacity and knowledge. Current conditions often inhibit positive actions and even encourage negative actions by businesses with harmful outcomes for biodiversity. Working collectively and individually actors can overcome barriers to positive action." ;
    dcterms:identifier "FigureSPM.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/BBA26-FigureSPM.3> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Impacts and dependencies of business on biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people. Businesses have impacts that can be direct indirect through the value chain or cumulative and their activities contribute to the five direct drivers of biodiversity loss. Their dependencies can be direct or through the value chain and can arise from nature’s material non?material and regulating contributions to people. Risks can be physical transitional or systemic. Opportunities for business include new products efficiency gains and increased resilience. Risks and opportunities may interact and risk management may create opportunities." ;
    dcterms:identifier "FigureSPM.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/BBA26-FigureSPM.4> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Examples of the intersections between impacts dependencies risks and opportunities " ;
    dcterms:identifier "FigureSPM.4" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/BBA26-FigureSPM.5> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Actions by businesses to address their impacts and dependencies and contribute to creating an enabling environment. At the corporate level businesses can set strategy design policies and management systems allocate capacity and resources introduce innovative processes products and services and explore alternative business models. These measures can facilitate actions at the operations value chain and portfolio levels and can contribute to creating an enabling environment. Business actions and the enabling environment continue to influence each other." ;
    dcterms:identifier "FigureSPM.5" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/BBA26-FigureSPM.6> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Examples of appropriate measurement approaches at different decision-making levels" ;
    dcterms:identifier "FigureSPM.6" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/BBA26-FigureSPM.A1> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The IPBES four-box model for qualitative communication of confidence. Confidence increases towards the top-right corner as suggested by the increasing strength of shading" ;
    dcterms:identifier "FigureSPM.A1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/BBA26-TableSPM.1> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Actions required to create an enabling environment. Actions organized by Governments (A) financial actors (B) businesses and financial institutions (C) and other actors (D) in the five dimensions of the enabling environment. Different actors may have different starting points and priorities and applicable actions may vary depending on evolving sectoral and national contexts priorities and capacity in accordance with national legislation." ;
    dcterms:identifier "TableSPM.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/BBA26-TableSPM.2> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Actions that businesses can take now to address their impacts and dependencies. Close links exist between actions implemented across the four decision-making levels. For example targets set at the corporate level drive actions and outcomes at the operations and value chain levels. Across all decision-making levels actions can be taken individually or collectively including through collaboration and partnerships. " ;
    dcterms:identifier "TableSPM.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/BBA26-TableSPM.3> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Suitability of methods for assessing impacts and dependencies by decision?making level and purpose of measurement. The categories of methods are not mutually exclusive and represent a continuum rather than strictly delineated categories. At each decision-making level methods can be applied for different purposes. The icons indicate the extent to which methods within each category are currently available and applicable can be applied given sufficient accuracy coverage and responsiveness (“Proceed with caution”) or are currently not feasible or applicable." ;
    dcterms:identifier "TableSPM.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/BBA26-TableSPM.4> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Examples of barriers to action across components of the enabling environment" ;
    dcterms:identifier "TableSPM.4" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/BBA26-TableSPM.5> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Examples of applications of methods for assessing business dependencies and impacts. Sociocultural and monetary valuation methods integrated approaches and accounting frameworks can also support assessment of dependencies and impacts {table 2.2 table 3.1 4.6}. " ;
    dcterms:identifier "TableSPM.5" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/BBA26-TableSPM.6> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Purposes of measurement and examples of applications" ;
    dcterms:identifier "TableSPM.6" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/BBA26-TableSPM.7> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Gaps in knowledge and its application" ;
    dcterms:identifier "TableSPM.7" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/BBA26-KM1> a ipbes:KeyMessage ;
    ipbes:BackgroundMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-A1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-A2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-A4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-A5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-A6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-A8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B7> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/BBA26> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people underpin the economy; all businesses therefore depend, directly or indirectly, on biodiversity. From 1820 to 2022, the global economy grew from $1.18 trillion to $130.11 trillion (measured in 2011 constant dollars). Businesses played a central role in that growth. However, the failure to account for nature and integrate its values into economic and financial systems has led to environmental degradation, unprecedented rates of biodiversity loss and associated changes in nature’s contributions to people, including ecosystem services, with 14 out of 18 categories of contributions measured showing declines. The benefits to people and the decline in biodiversity have been unequally distributed among and within countries. The decline in biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people has become a critical systemic risk to the economy, financial stability and human well-being, with implications for human rights. These systemic risks arising from biodiversity decline underscore the urgent need for transformative change. However, individual businesses often do not act to address their impacts and dependencies on biodiversity, or the resulting risks and opportunities, in part due to their lack of awareness. As businesses differ in size, sector, structure and relationship with biodiversity, informed action requires specific, context- and sector-dependent knowledge about impacts and dependencies. Because businesses are highly influential and can move quickly when motivated, informed and enabled to do so, they can be agents of positive change." ;
    dcterms:identifier "KM1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "All businesses depend on and impact biodiversity and can be agents of positive change {A1, A2, A4, A5, A6, A8, B7}." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/BBA26-KM10> a ipbes:KeyMessage ;
    ipbes:BackgroundMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-A8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B10>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B11>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B12>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B13>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B9> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/BBA26> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "		Key messages",
        """Under current conditions, what is profitable for businesses often results in loss of biodiversity, and what is good for biodiversity and society is often not profitable. Businesses are motivated to focus on financial materiality in order to meet short-term objectives and achieve profitability. This undermines their ability to take actions that address long-term outcomes with system-level implications. To address this problem, efforts have been made to require businesses to consider not only what is financially material but also what is material for biodiversity and society. While this can encourage businesses and financial institutions to consider multiple dimensions when taking decisions, it perpetuates a system built on the idea of trade-offs between profitability and biodiversity. Creating an enabling environment that provides incentives for the conservation and sustainable use of biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people could align what is profitable with what is good for biodiversity and society. It would result in businesses and financial institutions being positive agents of change in transforming to a just and sustainable economic system by addressing their impacts on biodiversity loss, climate change and pollution, which are interconnected. 
""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "KM10" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Creating an enabling environment can incentivize actions that are beneficial for businesses, biodiversity and society for a just and sustainable future {A8, B1, B2, B3, B4, B5, B6, B7, B8, B9, B10, B11, B12, B13}. ".
        

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/BBA26-KM2> a ipbes:KeyMessage ;
    ipbes:BackgroundMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-A1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-A2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-A3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-A5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B13>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C8> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/BBA26> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "While some businesses take actions that are beneficial for biodiversity, inadequate or perverse incentives perpetuate business as usual and create barriers to businesses and others taking action that could halt and reverse biodiversity loss and addressing its interconnection with climate change and pollution. The conditions under which different businesses operate vary and do not affect all sectors or businesses equally. Currently, the existing rewards and penalties are not adequate to drive sufficient action by businesses for the conservation and sustainable use of biodiversity and the fair and equitable sharing of benefits from the utilization of genetic resources and associated traditional knowledge. Businesses often fail to internalize the negative impacts of their actions on biodiversity. Many policies either encourage business activities that are harmful to biodiversity or prevent behaviour that is beneficial to it. For example, large subsidies are directed towards business activities that drive biodiversity loss, often with the support of lobbying by businesses and trade associations with vested interests. In 2023, global public and private financial flows with direct negative impacts on nature were estimated at $7.3 trillion, including environmentally harmful public subsidies and private investment in high-impact sectors. Of that total, private finance accounted for around two thirds ($4.9 trillion), while public spending on environmentally harmful subsidies made up the remaining $2.4 trillion. By contrast, only around $220 billion in public and private financial flows in 2023 were directed towards activities that contribute to the conservation and sustainable use of biodiversity. These figures highlight both the opportunity and the need to align financial flows to support biodiversity outcomes. Where compliance by businesses and enforcement efforts are lacking, the effective implementation of laws and regulations is undermined. Furthermore, because business disclosures are often voluntary and not widespread, businesses are usually not held accountable. Businesses often lack the data and knowledge to quantify their impacts and dependencies on biodiversity, and much of the relevant scientific literature is not written for a business audience. Lack of transparency across value chains, including with regard to the risks and opportunities related to the sustainability of resource extraction, use and reuse and waste management, is a further barrier to action. In addition, ecological processes such as ecosystem regeneration do not align with the time pressures on business decision-making and timescales for investment returns and reporting, which place heavy emphasis on quarterly earnings and annual reporting." ;
    dcterms:identifier "KM2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "The current external conditions in which businesses operate are not always compatible with achieving a just and sustainable future and perpetuate systemic risks {A1, A2, A3, A5, B7, B13, C8}." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/BBA26-KM3> a ipbes:KeyMessage ;
    ipbes:BackgroundMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B12>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B8> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/BBA26> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """In an enabling environment, the conditions and incentives under which businesses operate allow them to align their interests with what is beneficial for biodiversity and society. Creating an enabling environment involves changes in policy, legal and regulatory frameworks; economic and financial systems; social values, norms and culture; technology and data; and capacity and knowledge (figure SPM.2 and table SPM.1). These changes can be achieved by accelerating collaboration and collective action at all levels by and among Governments, financial actors, other actors, and businesses and financial institutions. Although collaboration is very important, individual actions can still contribute to creating an enabling environment.  Figure SPM.2. Creating an enabling environment would align what is beneficial for businesses with what is beneficial for biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people. Business activities impacting biodiversity outcomes are influenced by policy, legal and regulatory frameworks; economic and financial systems; social values, norms and culture; technology and data; and capacity and knowledge. Current conditions often inhibit positive actions and even encourage negative actions by businesses, with harmful outcomes for biodiversity. Working collectively and individually, actors can overcome barriers to positive action. Table SPM.1. Actions required to create an enabling environment. Actions organized by Governments (A), financial actors (B), businesses and financial institutions (C), and other actors (D) in the five dimensions of the enabling environment. Different actors may have different starting points and priorities, and applicable actions may vary depending on evolving sectoral and national contexts, priorities and capacity, in accordance with national legislation.     		 
""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "KM3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Collaboration, collective and individual actions are essential to creating an enabling environment in which businesses contribute to a just and sustainable future {B1, B2, B3, B4, B5, B6, B7, B8, B12}." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/BBA26-KM4> a ipbes:KeyMessage ;
    ipbes:BackgroundMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-A3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B10>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B11>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B12>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B13>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B9>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C1> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/BBA26> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """There are many actions that businesses, including financial institutions, can take now to benefit businesses and biodiversity (table SPM.2). The level of responsibility of individual businesses to act is a societal decision that can be informed by the scale of their impacts and dependencies on biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people, including ecosystem services. Methods exist for measuring businesses’ impacts and dependencies (table SPM.5). Impacts and dependencies can result in risks and opportunities for businesses. By understanding and managing their impacts and dependencies, businesses can reduce risks and deliver positive outcomes for both business and biodiversity. Understanding and managing impacts can lead to changes in business dependencies, and understanding dependencies can support actions to address impacts. Historically, business actions to manage negative impacts have mostly been driven by regulation (figure SPM.3). Businesses, including financial institutions, can: (a) establish corporate governance and strategic frameworks to set directions and enable actions that improve biodiversity outcomes across their operations, value chains and portfolio; (b) implement actions in their operations to deliver positive outcomes for biodiversity at the site, landscape and seascape levels, including by applying the mitigation hierarchy to avoid, minimize, restore, and offset impacts; (c) implement actions for their value chain that address impacts and dependencies directly or by influencing others upstream (working with suppliers) and downstream (engaging distributors, retailers and consumers); and (d) in the case of financial institutions, shift finance in their portfolios away from harmful activities and towards positive impacts. Through actions at these levels, businesses can contribute to an enabling environment and influence other actors to improve biodiversity outcomes. To avoid greenwashing, it is essential that businesses develop transparent and credible strategies based on an understanding of their impacts and dependencies and clearly demonstrate verifiable biodiversity outcomes (table SPM.2).  Figure SPM.3. Impacts and dependencies of business on biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people. Businesses have impacts that can be direct, indirect, through the value chain, or cumulative, and their activities contribute to the five direct drivers of biodiversity loss. Their dependencies can be direct or through the value chain and can arise from nature’s material, nonmaterial and regulating contributions to people. Risks can be physical, transitional or systemic. Opportunities for business include new products, efficiency gains and increased resilience. Risks and opportunities may interact, and risk management may create opportunities.
 Table SPM.2. Actions that businesses can take now to address their impacts and dependencies. Close links exist between actions implemented across the four decision-making levels. For example, targets set at the corporate level drive actions and outcomes at the operations and value chain levels. Across all decision-making levels, actions can be taken individually or collectively, including through collaboration and partnerships.  """ ;
    dcterms:identifier "KM4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "All businesses have a responsibility to address their impacts and dependencies {A3, B7, B8, B9, B10, B11, B12, B13, C1}." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/BBA26-KM5> a ipbes:KeyMessage ;
    ipbes:BackgroundMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-A5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C3> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/BBA26> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The science base for understanding and measuring business impacts and dependencies is already being used to guide business actions. Some businesses already apply existing methods, knowledge and data; as the science base improves, all businesses will be able to take action. The understanding of how to apply methods for impact assessment is more advanced than for assessments of dependencies. The availability of measurement approaches and underlying data varies by business sector, ecological realm and jurisdiction. The application and uptake of methods is low and uneven across and within business sectors and varies across regions and countries, reflecting differences in data availability and technical capacity. Different knowledge communities offer credible methodologies that can be applied across diverse business contexts, each contributing distinct approaches to addressing impacts and dependencies. Many aspects of biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people may be relevant to measuring business impacts and dependencies, including aspects at the genetic, species and ecosystem levels and the diverse benefits associated with nature’s contributions to people. Which aspects should be measured depends on the context, location and action to be taken or the decision to be informed. As a result, a combination of methods or metrics, which may include scientific, Indigenous and local knowledge, will often be necessary to fully capture the impacts and dependencies of business on biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people." ;
    dcterms:identifier "KM5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Methods, knowledge and data for measuring impacts and dependencies already exist and can further inform decisions and actions, directly and in the value chain {A5, B8, C1, C3}." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/BBA26-KM6> a ipbes:KeyMessage ;
    ipbes:BackgroundMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-A8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C7> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/BBA26> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "No single method is suitable for all business decisions (table SPM.3). Different methods and metrics are needed for distinct sectors, decision-making levels and purposes of measurement. Decisions at the operations level require site-specific information and data, which may be gathered using bottom-up approaches, including location-based observations, participatory mapping and monitoring, and spatial analysis built on the data from those sources. Such approaches can make use of data and metrics representing local values and the knowledge, rights and interests of other groups, including Indigenous Peoples and local communities. Bottom-up approaches can provide greater accuracy at local scales and can show how outcomes change in response to concrete actions or activities on the ground. Cumulative impacts, which arise from the aggregated effects of businesses and other actors at a location over time, are better assessed for an area such as a site, landscape or seascape than for a particular business. Top-down methods include life-cycle approaches and macro-scale environmental economic models appropriate for decisions at the portfolio, corporate and value chain levels. Depending on the purpose of measurement, they can be conducted with lower-resolution spatial data but wider geographical scale. Portfolio-level decisions can be guided by globally comparable methods where there is sufficient resolution within sectors (e.g., differentiating according to geography or product type). Corporate-level decisions can be informed by methods that use company-specific information. Value chain-level decisions can make use of life-cycle approaches that assess potential impacts of value chains, although they are less relevant for assessing dependencies. Top-down methods provide an assessment of potential impacts and dependencies and inform decisions through screening and comparing options. However, those methods may have limited ability to track progress resulting from specific business actions. Top-down approaches using coarse-resolution data often fail to reflect local ecological values and are less able to represent the diverse values of nature, including the values and interests of Indigenous Peoples and local communities. As a result, important areas for the future development of top-down approaches include higherresolution spatial and temporal data and better interweaving with diverse values of nature for understanding dependencies, impacts, risks and opportunities." ;
    dcterms:identifier "KM6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Different methods to measure and manage impacts and dependencies are needed for different sectors, decision-making levels and business purposes {A8, B8, C1, C2, C4, C7}." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/BBA26-KM7> a ipbes:KeyMessage ;
    ipbes:BackgroundMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C4> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/BBA26> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Methods can be assessed as fit for purpose against three overarching characteristics: coverage, accuracy and responsiveness. Coverage refers to both the geographical scale as well as the extent of impacts and dependencies included. To be fit for purpose, a method or combination of methods needs to cover the impacts and dependencies of the activities of a business at the relevant geographical scale. Accuracy is the degree to which the results correctly describe what they are designed to measure. The accuracy necessary for a method to be considered fit for purpose varies by both decision-making level and purpose of measurement. Responsiveness refers to the ability of the method to detect changes that can be attributed to the actions and activities of the business. To be fit for purpose, a method needs to clearly link changes in biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people to specific interventions.  Currently available methods do not always satisfy the criteria of coverage, accuracy and responsiveness for all business applications. Where methods do not meet the necessary requirements, they may not be entirely fit for purpose. They can, however, still produce useful information on which businesses can act. Such limitations need to be understood by businesses. Different methods also require different levels of expertise, resources and costs, presenting particular challenges for small and medium-sized enterprises. While recognizing constraints, businesses can start using methods and improve their capabilities over time.  Table SPM.3. Suitability of methods for assessing impacts and dependencies by decisionmaking level and purpose of measurement. The categories of methods are not mutually exclusive and represent a continuum rather than strictly delineated categories. At each decision-making level, methods can be applied for different purposes. The icons indicate the extent to which methods within each category are currently available and applicable, can be applied given sufficient accuracy, coverage and responsiveness (“Proceed with caution”), or are currently not feasible or applicable. " ;
    dcterms:identifier "KM7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Appropriate methods to measure and manage business impacts and dependencies can be selected on the basis of coverage, accuracy and responsiveness {C1, C2, C4}. " .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/BBA26-KM8> a ipbes:KeyMessage ;
    ipbes:BackgroundMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-A5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-A7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B10>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C8> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/BBA26> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Data and knowledge on the impacts and dependencies of business on biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people exist within the scientific community, among Indigenous Peoples and local communities, and within businesses, but are often siloed and not effectively considered by businesses in decision-making. Scientific literature is not written for businesses, and a lack of translation and attention to the needs of businesses has slowed uptake of scientific findings. Among businesses, there is often limited understanding of the role of Indigenous Peoples and local communities as stewards of biodiversity and holders of knowledge on its conservation, restoration and sustainable use. Better understanding could improve business decisionmaking. Indigenous Peoples and local communities often find themselves inadequately represented in research and decision-making processes, further undermining their contributions and weakening their voices, which may result in businesses failing to learn from them. This may have implications for the ability of businesses to comply with access and benefit-sharing mechanisms and approaches. While examples of interweaving Indigenous and local knowledge with scientific methods exist, businesses lack the experience of working with Indigenous Peoples and local communities needed to ensure that their knowledge is represented and their values are considered in decision-making. Due diligence by businesses involves consent mechanisms, including the free, prior and informed consent of Indigenous Peoples and local communities, in accordance with national legislation. Structured collaboration resulting in the sharing and better use of data and information, scientific insights and Indigenous and local knowledge on business impacts and dependencies can translate into better management of business risks and the realization of opportunities." ;
    dcterms:identifier "KM8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Businesses could better measure and manage their impacts and dependencies by appropriately engaging with science and Indigenous and local knowledge, methods and practices {A5, A7, B5, B6, B10, C3, C4, C7, C8}. " .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/BBA26-KM9> a ipbes:KeyMessage ;
    ipbes:BackgroundMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-A5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-A7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B13>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C8> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/BBA26> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Businesses can act now on the basis of existing knowledge; however, limitations remain in the currently available knowledge base and guidance. A paucity of quantitative, standardized and comparable estimates of the impacts of different businesses hinders the ability of all actors to fully understand and effectively manage business activities. This restricts the ability of financial institutions to choose investments that minimize negative or maximize positive impacts. Needs differ according to context, scale and sector, and consistent methodological approaches need to be developed and disseminated that produce accurate results, particularly for decision-making contexts in which the ability to compare or aggregate data across sites, value chains or businesses is important. Existing methods have yet to produce a comprehensive and comparable attribution of impacts across all sectors or businesses. The ability of assessments to inform action is hampered by their limited integration into business accounts and processes. Clear gaps in knowledge and its application exist in relation to: (a) business-relevant data; (b) completeness of evidence; (c) applicability of methods; (d) data accessibility and transparency; and (e) adoption of methods. Addressing these gaps would contribute to significantly improving the ability of businesses to measure their impacts and dependencies, with implications for their understanding of risks and opportunities. Better understanding could help to accelerate actions that benefit both businesses and biodiversity. Priority actions to address gaps can include: Facilitating greater uptake by businesses of existing methods and knowledge and their integration into business accounting and management systems; Collecting and sharing comparable information, including through disclosure mechanisms, on the activities and locations of businesses, their operations and value chains; Providing scenarios that support the assessment of performance against agreed goals and targets; Providing actionable guidance for businesses to select methods appropriate to their context and needs; Co-creating actions based on methods and knowledge built with participatory approaches involving Indigenous Peoples and local communities. Information that is easily understandable, standardized, transparent, interoperable, practical and actionable provides the foundation on which businesses and financial institutions can choose actions that are beneficial for biodiversity and society. Turning disclosure and awareness into informed action across the whole of society requires specific, context-dependent knowledge about business impacts and dependencies on biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people. In the case of dependencies, mandatory or voluntary disclosure could support scientific advances in order to accurately measure nature’s non-material and regulating contributions to people, which are currently underrepresented in tools and approaches. Examples include recreation, learning and inspiration, supporting identities, regulation of pollutants, climate regulation and pollination." ;
    dcterms:identifier "KM9" ;
    skos:prefLabel "The existing knowledge base needs to be strengthened by addressing important gaps in knowledge and its application {A5, A7, B5, B6, B13, C3, C4, C5, C6, C7, C8}. " .

